  "full_response": "Certainly! Below is a sample Terraform variables file named `variables.tf` that includes the Azure subscription ID, client secret, tenant ID, and storage account access key. This variable file allows you to store sensitive data in a secure and organized manner, without hardcoding these values into Terraform files.\n\nFilename: `variables.tf`\n\n```hcl\nvariable \"azure_subscription_id\" {\n  description = \"The Azure subscription ID of the resource provider.\"\n  type        = string\n  sensitive   = true\n}\n\nvariable \"azure_client_secret\" {\n  description = \"The Azure client secret for authentication.\"\n  type        = string\n  sensitive   = true\n}\n\nvariable \"azure_tenant_id\" {\n description = \"The Azure tenant ID for user authentication.\"\n  type        = string\n  sensitive   = true\n}\n\nvariable \"azure_storage_account_key\" {\n  description = \" Azure storage account access key.\"\n  type        = string\n  sensitive   = true\n}\n```\n\nPlease note that storing sensitive information even in a variables file is a security risk if the file includes plaintext values. It is highly recommended to use a secrets management tool (like HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or Azure Key Vault) for handling secrets instead of including them directly in Terraform files. As you add the actual values, make sure to avoid exposing them. Here is how you would reference these variables in your configuration:\n\nExample usage in a Terraform configuration (`main.tf`):\n\n```hcl\nprovider \"azurerm\" {\n  features {}\n  subscription_id = var.azure_subscription_id\n}\n\n# Example showing the use of the variable; replace with actual resource configurationresource \"azurerm_resource_group\" \"example\" {\n  name     = \"example-resources\"\n  location = \"East US\"\n  # client_id and client_secret are omitted for security reasons\n  // use a managed identity or other authentication methods for actual usage\n}\n```\n\nIn your respective environment (e.g., terminal), you would then pass the actual values to Terraform via command-line flags or a `.tfvars` file.\n\nIf using a `.tfvars` file, you would it like this (example: `terraform.tfvars`):\n\n```hcl\nazure_subscription_id = \"your-subscription-id\"\nazure_client_secret = \"your-client-secret\"\nazure_tenant_id      = \"your-tenant-id\"\nazure_storage_account_key = \"your-storage-account-key\"\n```\n\nBefore running any Terraform commands, ensure that you've securely stored these values in your `",
